{"records":[{"id":"bs3dnpr2g4","title":"Gasdermin D maintains bone mass by rewiring the endo-lysosomal pathway of osteoclastic bone resorption ","description":"Gasdermin D (GSDMD)-mediated pyroptosis induces immunogenic cell death and promotes inflammation. However, the functions of GSDMD in tissue homeostasis remain unclear. Here, we identify a physiological function of GSDMD in osteoclasts via a non-lytic p20 generated protein, which prevents bone loss to maintain bone homeostasis. In the late stage of RANKL-induced osteoclastogenesis, GSDMD underwent cleavage dependent on RIPK1 and caspase-8/-3 to yield this p20 product. Gsdmd-deficient osteoclasts showed normal differentiation but enhanced bone resorption with excessive lysosomal activity. Mice with complete or myeloid-specific Gsdmd deletion exhibited increased trabecular bone loss and more severe aging/ovariectomy-induced osteoporosis. GSDMD p20 was preferentially localized to early endosomes and limited endo-lysosomal trafficking and maturation, relying on its oligomerization and control of phosphoinositide conversion by binding to phosphatidylinositol 3-phosphate (PI(3)P). We have thus identified an anti-osteoclastic function of GSDMD as a checkpoint for lysosomal maturation and secretion, and linked this to bone homeostasis and endosome-lysosome biology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-23T20:42:12.308Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bs3dnpr2g4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bs3dnpr2g4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Di","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mobai","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dehang","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Lysosomes","Osteoclast","Endosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hmdttb83rd","title":"UPF1 promotes rapid degradation of m6A-containing RNAs. Boo et al.","description":"N6-methyladenosine (m6A) is the most prevalent internal modification in eukaryotic mRNAs and affects diverse RNA processing and metabolism. When YTHDF2, an m6A-recognizing protein, binds to m6A, it facilitates the destabilization of m6A-containing RNAs (m6A RNAs). Here, we demonstrate that upstream frameshift 1 (UPF1), a key factor for nonsense-mediated mRNA decay, interacts with YTHDF2, thereby triggering rapid degradation of m6A RNAs. The UPF1-mediated m6A RNA degradation depends on a specific interaction between UPF1 and N-terminal residues 101–168 of YTHDF2, UPF1 ATPase/helicase activities, and UPF1 interaction with proline-rich nuclear receptor coactivator 2 (PNRC2), a decapping-promoting factor preferentially involved in nonsense-mediated mRNA decay. Furthermore, transcriptome-wide analyses show that YTHDF2-bound mRNAs that are not substrates for HRSP12-RNase P/MRP–mediated endoribonucleolytic cleavage are destabilized with a higher dependency on UPF1. Collectively, our data indicate dynamic and multilayered regulation of the stability of m6A RNAs and highlight the multifaceted role of UPF1 in mRNA decay.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-09T13:04:29.539Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hmdttb83rd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hmdttb83rd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sung Ho","last_name":"Boo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yoon Ki","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Gel","Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8rss6rghvb","title":"Mechanisms of readthrough mitigation reveal principles of GCN1 mediated translational quality control","description":"Readthrough into the 3′ untranslated region (3′UTR) of the mRNA results in the production of aberrant proteins. Metazoans efficiently clear readthrough proteins, but the underlying mechanisms remain unknown. Here, we show in C. elegans and mammalian cells that readthrough proteins are targeted by a coupled, two-level quality control pathway involving the BAG6 chaperone complex and the ribosome collision-sensing protein GCN1. Readthrough proteins with hydrophobic C-terminal extensions are recognized by SGTA-BAG6 and ubiquitylated by RNF126 for proteasomal degradation. Additionally, cotranslational mRNA decay initiated by GCN1 and CCR4/NOT limits the accumulation of readthrough products. Unexpectedly, selective ribosome profiling uncovered a general role of GCN1 in regulating translation dynamics when ribosomes collide at nonoptimal codons, enriched in 3′UTRs, transmembrane proteins and collagens. Dysfunction of GCN1 increasingly perturbs these protein classes during aging, resulting in mRNA and proteome imbalance. Our results define GCN1 as a key factor acting during translation in maintaining protein homeostasis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-16T15:34:25.996Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8rss6rghvb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8rss6rghvb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Mueller","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gopal","last_name":"Jayaraj","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ulrich","last_name":"Hartl","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Microscopy","Flow Cytometry","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mh32mymh62","title":"Pharmacologic inhibition of nonsense-mediated decay induces anti-tumour immunogenicity in ex vivo patient tumours","description":"Data and code for: Pharmacologic inhibition of nonsense-mediated decay induces anti-tumour immunogenicity in ex vivo patient tumours","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-05T06:29:38.029Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mh32mymh62","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mh32mymh62"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Roberto","last_name":"Vendramin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biomedical Research"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xfs5tpbx6j","title":"original data of METTL5-mediated 18S rRNA m6A modification promotes oncogenic mRNA translation and intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma progression","description":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (ICC) is a highly lethal malignancy characterized by rapid tumor progression. Hyperactive mRNA translation driven by mis-regulated RNA modifications has emerged as an essential oncogenic mechanism in ICC. While mRNA and tRNA modifications have been intensively studied, the function and mechanism of ribosomal RNA (rRNA) modifications, however, remain poorly understood in cancer biology. \n18S rRNA m6A is one of the two m6A modification in human rRNA catalyzed by methyltransferase METTL5. Despite its emerging functional importance in regulation of stem cell fate and growth, the role of 18S rRNA m6A modification in cancers remains largely unclear. Here we found that 18S rRNA m6A modification and METTL5 are aberrantly upregulated in ICC and associate with poor clinical survival. We further revealed the critical role of METTL5-mediated 18S rRNA m6A modification in regulation of ICC cell growth and metastasis using loss- and gain-of function assays in vitro and in vivo. The crucial oncogenic function of METTL5 was further corroborated using liver-specific knockout and overexpression ICC mouse models. Mechanistically, METTL5 depletion impairs 18S rRNA m6A modification level that hampers ribosome synthesis, which preferentially results in the translation inhibition of G-quadruplexes-containing mRNAs that enriched in many oncogenic signals including TGF-beta pathway. \nThere are two novel findings in this original research. First, we disclose the important physiological functions of METTL5-mediated 18S rRNA m6A modification in ICC, uncovering an important link between rRNA modifications and cancer development. Second, we find that METTL5-mediated 18S rRNA m6A modification preferentially modulate the translation of certain oncogenic transcripts containing G-quadruplexes and shorter 5’-UTR length due to the impaired ribosome synthesis. Excepting mRNA and tRNA modifications, our study reveals an additional layer of selective regulation of gene expression mediated by rRNA modification, providing new strategies targeting abnormal translation for effective ICC treatment from rRNA modification layer. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-06T19:28:01.452Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xfs5tpbx6j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xfs5tpbx6j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zihao","last_name":"Dai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"wanjie","last_name":"zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"yingdong","last_name":"hou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"zhang","last_name":"xinyue","orcid":null},{"first_name":"xuxin","last_name":"ren","orcid":null},{"first_name":"kai","last_name":"lei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junbin","last_name":"Liao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haining","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"zhihang","last_name":"chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sui","last_name":"Peng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"shaoqiang","last_name":"li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuibin","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ming","last_name":"Kuang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["File Backup"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cp8cvzx22z","title":"OPA1 promotes ferroptosis by augmenting mitochondrial ROS and suppressing an integrated stress response","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of nonapoptotic cell death mediated by lipid peroxidation, has been implicated in the pathogenesis of multiple diseases. Subcellular organelles play pivotal roles in the regulation of ferroptosis, but the mechanisms underlying the contributions of the mitochondria remain poorly defined. Optic atrophy 1 (OPA1) is a mitochondrial dynamin-like GTPase that controls mitochondrial morphogenesis, fusion, and energetics. Here, we report that human and mouse cells lacking OPA1 are markedly resistant to ferroptosis. Reconstitution with OPA1 mutants demonstrates that ferroptosis sensitization requires the GTPase activity but is independent of OPA1-mediated mitochondrial fusion. Mechanistically, OPA1 confers susceptibility to ferroptosis by maintaining mitochondrial homeostasis and function, which contributes both to the generation of mitochondrial lipid reactive oxygen species (ROS) and suppression of an ATF4-mediated integrated stress response. Together, these results identify an OPA1-controlled mitochondrial axis of ferroptosis regulation and provide mechanistic insights for therapeutically manipulating this form of cell death in diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-13T17:28:29.217Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cp8cvzx22z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cp8cvzx22z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Felix ","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5gyvf6nf53","title":"Raw data for \"A mycobacterial Sec61 inhibitor disrupts lysosome function by blocking Vacuolar-ATPase biosynthesis\"","description":"This dataset contains the raw confocal and western blot images for the publication \"A mycobacterial Sec61 inhibitor disrupts lysosome function by blocking Vacuolar-ATPase biosynthesis\", Hall et al. European Journal of Cell Biology, 2026 which describes the impact of the bacterial toxin mycolactone on lysosomal function. Mycolactone inhibits the Sec61 translocon, causing mislocalisation of membrane and secretory proteins to the cytosol. Cells respond by upregulating the proteasome and autophagy pathways but the down regulation of the V-ATPase accessory proteins ATP6AP1 and ATP6AP2 leads to progressive loss of acidic lysosomes, limiting the ability of cells to restore homeostasis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-28T17:00:09.599Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5gyvf6nf53","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5gyvf6nf53"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Belinda","last_name":"Hall","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2m74kh2m2y","title":"Alternative Polyadenylation Determines the Functional Landscape of Inverted Alu Repeats. Ku et al.","description":"Inverted Alu repeats (IRAlus) are abundantly found in the transcriptome, especially in introns and 3′ UTRs. Yet, the biological significance of IRAlus embedded in 3′ UTRs remains largely unknown. Here, we find that 3′ UTR IRAlus silence genes involved in essential signaling pathways. We utilize J2 antibody to directly capture and map the double-stranded RNA structure of 3′ UTR IRAlus in the transcriptome. Bioinformatic analysis reveals alternative polyadenylation as a major axis of IRAlus-mediated gene regulation. Notably, the expression of mouse double minute 2 (MDM2), an inhibitor of p53, is upregulated by the exclusion of IRAlus during UTR shortening, which is exploited to silence p53 during tumorigenesis. Moreover, the transcriptome-wide UTR lengthening in neural progenitor cells results in the global downregulation of genes associated with neurodegenerative diseases, including amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, via IRAlus inclusion. Our study establishes the functional landscape of 3′ UTR IRAlus and its role in human pathophysiology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-27T19:58:36.345Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2m74kh2m2y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2m74kh2m2y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jayoung","last_name":"Ku","orcid":"0000-0002-4112-4582"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein P53","Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis","Gene Regulation","Long Noncoding RNA"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mwj2cc9kpy","title":"Diehl et al_2026_rRNA decay","description":"This study shows that oligo(uridine) tailing of 18S rRNA triggers DIS3L2-mediated decay, resulting in 40S ribosome degradation in response to stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-12T14:16:23.686Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mwj2cc9kpy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mwj2cc9kpy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Frances","last_name":"Diehl","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ribosomal RNA","Ribosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5t6r2mvt9j","title":"Deciphering 3’ UTR mediated gene regulation using interpretable deep representation learning","description":"Source code, documentation, and instruction for 3UTRBERT. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-08T06:28:55.797Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5t6r2mvt9j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5t6r2mvt9j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"YUNING","last_name":"YANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Language Processing","Sequence Analysis","Deep Learning"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":130916,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":113,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":130916,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":130299,"restricted":66,"embargoed":551},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2208},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1445},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":671},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":452},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":399},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":389},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":333},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":264}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":128351},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1845},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":183},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":132},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":39},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":934},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":925},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":482},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":358}]}}